      "textEn": "The scattered rays constitute the diffuse light. This is confirmed by Mundaka (2.2.10), Katha (2.2.15)\nand Shrutashatara (6.14) also. Neither the sun shines there, nor the light, nor the sun's light.\n\nNeither the light of the stars illuminates, nor is fire.\n\nThat itself illuminates all\n\nHis light illuminates all this.\n\nIn the abode of God, there is no need for the sun, moon, or stars for light. Nor is there a need for electricity there. So, what can be said about the lamp? On the other hand, in these worlds,\nbecause of their self-illuminating nature, all the radiance is possible, and whatever is radiant there is due to the reflection of that\nself-sacrifice. He who is bewildered by the all-pervading Brahman-light cannot know the Supreme Lord himself. Therefore, in the Inshopanishad (15), this prayer has gone that God should remove his miraculous radiance,\nso that the devotee can have a vision of reality. This verse is as follows--\n\nHiranmayena patrena satyasyaapihitamamukham.\nYou are the one who, for the sake of truth and righteousness, has been seen by the eyes. “Oh, my God! You are the protector of all physical and spiritual things, and every object\ngrows and flourishes by your grace. Your devotion is the true principle of religion, that is, the true dharma.\nAnd I am engaged in that service. Please protect me by showing your true face. Therefore,\nyou remove this veil of your Brahmajyoti rays, so that I can see your Sachchidananda form.''"
