      "textEn": "776\nEven great scholars and great followers of great Vedic principles cannot attain such great qualities\nwithout dedicating them to the service of God.\n\nMeaning: Progress in knowledge, charity, political, social, or religious leadership of human society,\nphilosophical contemplation, mastery of yoga practice, and all such excellent qualities of man are only helpful in his attainment of perfection when they are used in the service of God. Without doing this, all these qualities become a cause of suffering for man. Every object can be used\neither for one's own gratification or for the service of others in addition to oneself. There are also two types of selfishness--personal selfishness and extended selfishness. But in these two types of selfishness,\nthere is no qualitative difference. Whether theft is done for personal gain or for family gain,\nit is the same--that is, criminal.\nIf a thief, not for himself, but for the benefit of society or\nthe country, declares himself innocent for committing theft, then by the law of any country\nhe cannot be forgiven. Ordinary people do not have the knowledge that the selfishness of a living being is only then\nfulfilled when such selfishness is inseparable from the selfishness of God. For example, what is the selfishness in nurturing the body and\nsoul together? A human earns money to maintain the body (private or\nsocial), but until there is divine consciousness, until the body is maintained with God\nuntil one realizes the feeling of one's relationship, all efforts to maintain the body and soul together\nare similar to the body and soul being maintained by an animal. The purpose of human body's\nnurturing is different from animals.\nSimilarly, the progress of knowledge, economic development,\nPhilosophical research, the study of Vedic literature, or performing virtuous deeds (such as charity, opening a hospital,\nopening an annadan (food donation), all these tasks should be related to God. The purpose of all such actions and efforts\nshould be to please God, not to satisfy any power, person, or group (Siddhirahari\nToshanam). The same principle is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (9.27) where it is said that\nwhatever charity we give and whatever penance we perform, all should be offered to God or to Him"
